Let $f(z)$ be regular for $|z| < R$;

$$\mathscr{G}(r) = e^{\frac{1}{2\pi} \int_0^{2\pi} \log |f(re^{i\theta})| d\theta}$$

denotes the geometric mean of $|f(z)|$ on the circle $|z| = r$, $r < R$. The function $\mathscr{G}(r)$ is monotone increasing with $r$ and a convex function of log $r$ (in the wide sense).

solution:
Assume $\varepsilon > 0$; the function $\varphi(z) = \frac{e^z}{[f(z)]^\varepsilon}$ is regular in the half-plane $\Re z \geq 0$. We have

$$|\varphi(z)| \leq 1 \text{ for } \Re z = 0 \text{ and } |\varphi(z)| \leq 1 \text{ for } |z| = r,$$

if $r$ is so large that $\omega(r) > \frac{1}{\varepsilon}$, hence $|\varphi(z)| \leq 1$ in the entire half-plane $\Re z \geq 0$; and finally, as $\varepsilon \to 0$, $|e^z| \leq 1$: contradiction. (Borderline case of 290: The region $\mathfrak{T}$ becomes a halfplane.)
